# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Each item: (id, question, say, show, pushed)
SECTIONS=[
("A","AI usage","30%","Is AI central, demonstrated appropriately and within limits?",[
("A1","Where exactly is the AI? Isn’t this a dashboard with a chatbot?",
 "The AI sits at five layers of the design, and three steps are live today: cited summaries, questions about one passport, and plain-English search for administrators. The hard problem in this product is not storing records, it is understanding them across messy systems: whose record is this, which skill does it show, what does this PDF say, and how do I explain it. Rules cannot cover course titles and formats we have not seen. A dashboard only shows what someone already wired up.",
 "Slide 6 (AI by layer), then the chat in Live AI mode.",
 "Matching and mapping run on rules in the prototype. The architecture isolates those steps so a model can plug in behind human approval. We are showing real AI where it is live and saying plainly where rules stand in."),
("A2","Why do you need AI at all? Couldn’t rules or search do this?",
 "For anything known in advance, rules are better, and we use them: statuses and dates are computed by code, never by the model. AI earns its place in three jobs rules do badly: mapping an unseen course title or document to our seven skill areas, fuzzy matching of people across name and email variants, and understanding free-form questions and explaining results in plain language.",
 "Switch the chat to Offline rules and ask a question with unusual wording. Then switch to Live AI and ask the same thing.",
 "Offline rules only handle the question types we wrote. That limit is the reason a model is needed."),
("A3","How do you stop the AI from making things up?",
 "Five layers. The model only sees that person’s structured records, not the open web. The prompt requires a record ID after every claim and an answer of ‘not on file’ when the data is silent. The page then checks every answer: every sentence cited, every ID real, no scoring language. The dates and statuses in the interface come from code, not from the model. And every citation is one click from the source record.",
 "The green check chips under an answer. Click a record chip such as MO-09 to open the source record.",
 "Be honest about the limit: the checks prove a citation exists and is valid, not that the sentence is semantically right. Before a pilot we would add claim-level checks against the cited record and an evaluation set written with educators."),
("A4","Is the AI scoring or ranking clinicians? What about the EU AI Act?",
 "No, by design. We show evidence: current, due soon, lapsed or no evidence, with recency and volume. We never rate performance. Ask it to rank people and it refuses. The reason is practical and legal: AI that evaluates staff or learning outcomes can be high-risk under the EU AI Act, and those obligations are currently postponed to December 2027 according to legal summaries. We would get legal review before any scoring. The AHA 2025 guidelines also list consistent passing standards and teamwork measurement as open gaps, so a score would be invented precision.",
 "Admin chat: ‘Rank the nurses’. Learner chat: ‘Is this person the best in the unit?’ Both are refused.",
 "A descriptive tool can still be misused for employment decisions. We would document the intended use, keep the view transparent to the learner, and take it through legal and privacy review. Confirm the AI Act dates with Laerdal legal before quoting them as fact."),
("A5","What data goes to the model? What about privacy?",
 "Per question the model sees the person’s role, unit, time in role, skill-area statuses and their records: titles, sources, dates and validity. No name, no email, no employee ID. For the administrator search the model sees only the question and the list of allowed filter values. The app runs the search itself, so no user data leaves. In production the calls would go through the company’s approved AI services, with data-residency terms and a privacy impact assessment.",
 "The ‘No names or emails sent’ check chip. Slide 9.",
 "Do not say ‘anonymous’. Role plus unit plus dates could identify someone in a small team, so we call it pseudonymous. Confirm the production route with the AI Services team."),
("A6","What if a PDF or record contains malicious text that tries to instruct the model (prompt injection)?",
 "Text from records is treated as data, never as instructions, and the prompt says so. The model only receives structured fields. It has no tools and cannot change anything: it reads and narrates. For documents, extraction is forced into a fixed schema and a person reviews the fields before anything is saved. Outputs go through the same checks.",
 "Architecture slide: ‘Files and PDFs’ box and the human-review step.",
 "We have not red-teamed it yet. That is on the plan before any pilot, with injection test documents."),
("A7","How do you measure the AI’s accuracy?",
 "We have not measured accuracy yet and we will not quote a number we do not have. What exists today is logic tests on the guardrails: invalid IDs are stripped, uncited sentences and scoring language are flagged, and refusals work. The plan is an evaluation set of passports with educator-written expected answers, measuring citation validity, factual agreement, refusal rate on scoring prompts, and field accuracy for document extraction.",
 "Slide 17 (what we manage) or the plan on slide 16.",
 "If they want a threshold, say it will be agreed with clinical educators, because the acceptable error for a refresher reminder differs from a credential decision."),
("A8","Which model do you use? What about lock-in and cost?",
 "The prototype calls a Claude model through the hosted page, on the fast tier, with short prompts that carry one person’s records. The prompts, checks and data model do not depend on the model, so production can sit behind an approved gateway and swap models. Calls happen only when someone asks, never in the background. We have not measured cost per answer yet. We will in a pilot.",
 "Slide 12 (detailed design, AI services).",
 "Do not quote a price per call. Say what the design controls: small context, on-demand, cacheable."),
("A9","The name says ‘Agent’. Is this really an agent?",
 "Today it is an assistant: it reads records, answers questions and turns a question into a filter. It takes no actions on its own, deliberately, because this is people’s training records. The agent steps we plan are proactive expiry nudges, suggested refreshers and drafted reminders, each approved by a person before anything is sent.",
 "Slide 16 roadmap.",
 "If asked why not fully autonomous: the cost of a wrong autonomous action on staff records is higher than the time saved."),
("A10","Your sources are not decided. How does AI help you take in PDFs and unknown formats?",
 "That is exactly where AI helps. A text PDF can be read directly. A scan or screenshot goes through OCR, which turns the picture into text. A model then pulls out the fields we need, such as course, date, expiry and person, into our common record. Anything uncertain goes to a review queue, and a person confirms before it enters the passport.",
 "Detailed design: ‘Files and PDFs’ and ‘Source intake’ boxes.",
 "This is planned, not built. The prototype uses synthetic records in the common format."),
("A11","If the AI summary is wrong, who is accountable?",
 "The summary never changes the record and never makes a decision. It is a reading aid with citations, so the reader can check any claim in one click, and the educator remains the decision-maker. In production we would keep an audit log of what the model saw and said, and show learners the same view.",
 "Click a citation chip to open the underlying record.",
 "Add the plan: claim-level checks and educator review of a sample before pilot."),
("A12","Where is the human in the loop?",
 "Four places. A weak identity match, such as one found on a personal email only, waits for a person. A skill mapping proposed by AI is approved by an educator. Document extraction is reviewed before saving. And no AI output changes a record or scores anyone.",
 "Slide 6, column ‘A person decides’. The pending-match notice on Daniel Reyes’s passport.",
 "In the prototype the matching and mapping use rules, and the approval screens come after the MVP."),
]),
("B","User value and business impact","20%","Clear value, visible effect on cost, time, quality or satisfaction.",[
("B1","Who is the user, and what is their pain?",
 "Four audiences. Educators and administrators chase records across several systems and find expiring skills late. Learners have no single view of what they have shown. Institutions need readiness evidence for audits and accreditation. Laerdal gets a measurable-outcomes story beyond individual products. The underlying pain is simple: skills fade within months, and the evidence is scattered.",
 "Slide 3 and slide 14.",
 "We have worked from public programme knowledge and guidelines, not a structured interview round yet. Say so if asked."),
("B2","What is the measurable impact?",
 "We have not measured impact yet, and we are not going to invent numbers. We have four hypotheses to test in a pilot: time to prepare a readiness view, days between a record expiring and someone noticing, share of expiring credentials renewed on time, and educator and learner satisfaction. We would take a baseline first, then compare.",
 "Slide 14, right-hand panel ‘What we will measure in a pilot’.",
 "If pressed for a guess, say that you will not guess, and that the first pilot task is the baseline. Credibility beats a made-up percentage."),
("B3","How does this ‘automate the everyday’?",
 "Today an educator logs into several systems, compares dates and compiles a list by hand. With CORA the question ‘who has something expiring in 30 days?’ is one sentence, and the answer comes from all sources at once. The summary of a person’s evidence is written automatically with citations, and expiring skills are visible without anyone looking for them.",
 "Admin chat: ‘Who has something expiring in 30 days?’ then ‘NICU staff with CPR lapsed’.",
 "The time saved is a hypothesis we will measure, not a claim."),
("B4","What is the business model? Who pays?",
 "That is not decided and it should be tested with customers. Options we see: an add-on to existing programme and simulation subscriptions, or an institution-level licence for cross-programme readiness. The strategic value for Laerdal is moving the conversation from courses delivered to skills evidenced, across AHA and AAP programmes.",
 "Slide 14, Laerdal card.",
 "A pilot at one training centre is also how we learn willingness to pay."),
("B5","What is in it for Laerdal specifically?",
 "It connects data Laerdal already generates, such as simulator and RQI data and SimCapture sessions, with partner programmes into one view of readiness. It differentiates from generic competency suites through objective, simulator-derived evidence. And it deepens the AHA and AAP relationships by making their programmes’ results visible in one place.",
 "Slide 15.",
 "Frame it as complementing existing products, not competing with them."),
("B6","Have you spoken to real users?",
 "Not in a structured way yet. We built from the public programme documentation and the AHA 2025 guidelines, and from how the programmes issue and expire evidence. The first pilot step is interviews with educators and nurse managers, and we are asking for introductions to them.",
 "Slide 17, ‘What we need’.",
 "Never imply interviews that did not happen."),
("B7","Would clinicians feel monitored? What would make a hospital adopt it?",
 "We designed against that: no scores, no rankings, a learner sees their own passport, and every statement links to its source. The framing is support, not surveillance. For adoption, it does not replace any system, so there is little to rip out. It can start with one or two sources and show value in the first week through the expiry view.",
 "Constellation view and the guardrails slide.",
 "Consent and visibility screens come after the MVP, so do not claim they exist. Co-design with nurse managers is the plan."),
("B8","Does this improve patient outcomes?",
 "We do not claim that. The guidelines link skill decay and exposure to outcomes at a population level, and our hypothesis is that seeing lapsing skills earlier supports readiness. Patient outcomes are not something a hackathon prototype can show, and a pilot would measure readiness measures first.",
 "Slide 3 source note.",
 "The exposure evidence is observational and mostly from EMS settings. Say that."),
]),
("C","Domain and product relevance","20%","How relevant is the output to our domain and products?",[
("C1","How does this relate to our existing products?",
 "It sits on top of them. The sources are the systems and programmes Laerdal and its partners already run: RQI1Stop and the RQI programmes, AHA provider cards and HeartCode, PediaSkills for AAP’s NRP and PEPP, SimCapture simulation sessions, and local learning systems. We do not replace any of them. We read what they produce and add one identity across programmes.",
 "Slide 15 and the architecture sources column.",
 "Record formats shown are assumptions until the source owners confirm them."),
("C2","Doesn’t SimCapture or RQI Analytics already do this?",
 "Each is strong inside its own product. RQI Analytics reports compliance and competence for RQI programmes, and SimCapture tracks competency within simulation, including AI features. Neither is designed to show one clinician across AHA cards, AAP courses, local learning records and unit logs together. CORA would consume their data through exports or APIs and could reuse their outputs rather than duplicate them.",
 "Slide 15, the ‘Complements, not replaces’ band.",
 "We have not reviewed those products’ internal roadmaps. Say that we want to align with those teams early to avoid duplication."),
("C3","What about competency suites from HealthStream or Relias?",
 "They manage competency and learning records for healthcare employers, and they describe AI features and audit support. Our differentiator is evidence that comes from simulators and Laerdal programmes, plus the AHA and AAP ecosystem. We could also ingest their data as another source instead of competing, and that is open.",
 "Slide 15.",
 "Those are vendor claims. Do not describe their products beyond that."),
("C4","Why these seven skill areas?",
 "They come from the competency areas in the original idea and map to resuscitation and emergency programmes: CPR, airway, patient assessment, emergency response, medication administration, communication and teamwork, and maternal and neonatal emergencies. The taxonomy is data, so educators can add or change areas, and a role profile decides which apply to whom. A nurse in the emergency department is not expected to show maternal skills, and that is not shown as a gap.",
 "Constellation view: Maya’s maternal area is ‘Outside role profile’.",
 "The mapping needs validation with clinical educators and against frameworks such as AHA, ERC and national nursing standards."),
("C5","Is this grounded in clinical evidence?",
 "Yes, in three points from the AHA 2025 guidelines on resuscitation education. CPR skills can decline within about three months. Monitoring exposure to real resuscitation events is recommended for EMS systems. And consistent mastery standards and teamwork measurement are open gaps, which is why we show evidence and recency instead of ratings.",
 "Slide 3 and the source footnote.",
 "The exposure finding is observational. Recent exposure, not years on the job, tracked with outcomes. Do not overstate it."),
("C6","How do NRP and PEPP fit?",
 "They are two of our sources through PediaSkills. NRP covers neonatal resuscitation, including the quarterly RQI-for-NRP programme, and PEPP is AAP’s prehospital paediatric course. Its fifth edition launched in September 2026 with AAP and RQI Partners. We map them to maternal and neonatal, airway, assessment and emergency areas.",
 "Priya Nair’s passport (paramedic) uses PEPP and NEPP; Daniel Reyes uses NRP.",
 "The data shape for these is assumed until PediaSkills confirms how records can be shared. AAP does not share status with third parties without consent."),
("C7","Is this a credential? What is its regulatory status?",
 "It is not a credential and it certifies no one. Credentials stay with the programmes, such as AHA and AAP cards. CORA describes the evidence on file. It makes no clinical decisions and the prototype holds no patient data.",
 "Footer line on the poster: ‘Not a credential’.",
 "Intended use must be documented, and legal and privacy review comes before any pilot."),
("C8","Does it use simulator data, which is Laerdal’s strength?",
 "In the design, yes: SimCapture sessions and observer checklists, and manikin metrics such as compression depth, rate and recoil, appear in the evidence record. That objective evidence is what a generic learning system does not have.",
 "Open any RQI record: the drawer shows source-reported metrics.",
 "The prototype uses synthetic metrics. Real simulator data access is a next step."),
]),
("D","Feasibility and reuse","10%","Can it be implemented, and can it be reused?",[
("D1","Is this feasible? How would you build it?",
 "In phases, starting with one or two sources. Source adapters take in data, whether events, API pulls or documents. Everything becomes one common evidence record, stored in PostgreSQL with JSONB for variable fields. Passport views are rebuilt when mappings change, and an API with access control serves the learner, admin and AI services. The prototype proves the interface, the data model and the AI interaction pattern.",
 "Slides 11 and 12.",
 "The unknowns are data access and identity, not the AI."),
("D2","What is the hardest part?",
 "Getting the data: formats, access and consent for each source, and matching the same person across systems. The AI part is comparatively tractable. That is why intake is deliberately flexible, and why we would start the pilot with the sources that give us data first.",
 "Detailed design: ‘Not decided yet’ box.",
 "Name the open questions you need answered: delivery method, stable user ID, organisation ID, backfill."),
("D3","You do not know your sources yet. How can you call this feasible?",
 "Because the value is in the layer, not in any one connector. Whatever a source gives us, whether events, an API, a file export or a PDF, ends up as the same common record. Adding a source means adding a registry row and an adapter, not rebuilding the product. We would pilot with whichever one or two sources we can access first.",
 "Slide 13: ‘Extend by data, not code’.",
 "Be clear that the decision on delivery routes is open and is one of the first asks."),
("D4","What if a source changes its format?",
 "Adapters are versioned. A record with an unknown schema goes to a review queue instead of into the passport. Raw input is saved first, so after a fix we can replay it. Every evidence record notes which mapping version produced it, so changes are traceable.",
 "Detailed design: queue, dead-letter queue, raw event store.",
 "Not built in the prototype. It is a design decision."),
("D5","How would it scale?",
 "Events and files are processed idempotently, so duplicates are harmless, and status comes from record time, not arrival order. Passport views are precomputed and rebuilt only when mappings change. AI calls happen only on demand, on one person’s records at a time.",
 "Detailed design: ‘Idempotent’ note.",
 "We have not load-tested. Those are design choices, not benchmarks."),
("D6","How reusable is it beyond this case?",
 "New sources, skills, user fields and role rules are added as data: registry rows, taxonomy rows, attribute definitions and role profiles. The same layer can serve AHA and AAP programmes and other Laerdal systems, and the pattern applies beyond resuscitation to any skill with evidence and expiry.",
 "Slide 13.",
 "Keep the claim to what the design supports."),
("D7","How do you match the same person across systems? Email seems weak.",
 "It is a weak key on its own, which is why we layer it. A work email, or work plus personal, matches automatically. A personal email alone waits for a person, and the learner can prove ownership through a claim link sent to that mailbox. Ambiguous matches go to review and are never auto-merged. Emails are stored hashed in records. Our roadmap adds a stable source user ID.",
 "Daniel Reyes’s passport: one record is waiting for identity confirmation and is not counted.",
 "Leavers and address changes need a rule. That is on the list of questions for the source owners."),
("D8","Security and tenancy?",
 "The design puts a tenant ID on every table with row-level security, role-based access in front of everything, hashed emails with the plain text in a separate vault, and an audit trail. The tenant model is not decided: one organisation per deployment or many.",
 "Detailed design: PostgreSQL, PII vault, API and access control.",
 "The prototype’s sign-in is mocked with demo accounts. Say so."),
]),
("E","Working prototype","10%","A working prototype is shown, with sample data if required.",[
("E1","What actually works today?",
 "Sign-in for learners and administrators, the passport with the constellation, timeline and source records, the AI chat with demo questions in Live AI or Offline rules mode, guardrail checks on every answer, the administrator user list with search, filters and AI search, three visual themes, and twelve synthetic clinicians with 98 records from six sources.",
 "Run the three-minute demo path on slide 10.",
 "Offer the list of what is mocked before they ask."),
("E2","Is the data real?",
 "No. Every clinician and record is synthetic. The source names are real systems, but the record shapes are our assumptions until integrations are confirmed. Twelve people, 98 records: 95 are counted and three are waiting for an identity match.",
 "Footer text on the prototype and poster.",
 "Do not describe any record as real training data."),
("E3","Is the AI really running live, or is it scripted?",
 "In Live AI mode it is a real model call: the answers are not scripted, and you can type any question. The label on every answer says whether it came from the live model or from offline rules, so there is no ambiguity. The first use asks the viewer for permission, because it uses their own account.",
 "Ask a question you have not rehearsed in Live AI mode.",
 "If the model is slow or unavailable, the chat falls back to rules and says so. Never present an offline answer as AI output."),
("E4","Is the login real?",
 "It is a prototype sign-in with demo accounts to show the two roles, learner and administrator. Nothing is authenticated. A real deployment would use the organisation’s single sign-on.",
 "Sign-in screen note.",
 "Do not overstate security."),
("E5","Show me a case where it fails safely.",
 "Ask ‘Rank the nurses’ as administrator or ‘Is this person the best in the unit?’ as learner: it refuses to rank. Ask an unsupported question in Offline rules and it lists what it can answer. If the model fails mid-demo, it falls back to rules with a visible note.",
 "The two refusal questions on slide 8.",
 "This is the clearest way to demonstrate ‘within limits’."),
("E6","Why did you leave out the consent screens and the team view?",
 "We kept the MVP focused on the passport, the AI assistant and the administrator list. A consent and sources screen and a team readiness view are designed and marked ‘later’ in the architecture. The code for both is kept behind a switch so they can be turned back on.",
 "Slide 16, ‘Later’ column.",
 "Consent is an important part of the privacy story, so be honest that it is not shown yet."),
]),
("F","Poster and presentation","10%","Problem, value and output articulated, including the AI usage.",[
("F1","Explain it in one sentence.",
 "CORA builds one living clinical identity for each clinician from the training systems they already use: seven skill areas showing what is current, expiring or missing, explained by AI with citations, and never scored.",
 "The poster title block.",
 "Practise this until it takes ten seconds."),
("F2","Why call it a passport if it is not a credential?",
 "A passport is a portable record of where you have been, with stamps and expiry dates. It carries evidence, not a licence. The licence or card stays with the programme that issued it.",
 "Poster header.",
 "If this confuses people, say ‘evidence passport’."),
("F3","What does CORA stand for?",
 "Competency Optimization Readiness Agent: your clinical competency passport.",
 "Slide footer.",
 "Keep it to one line."),
("F4","What do you need from us to take this forward?",
 "Five things: introductions to the owners of RQI1Stop, SimCapture and PediaSkills; sample or test data and a decision on how each source delivers it; approval for the AI route through the company’s AI services; a small panel of educators to validate the skill mapping; and privacy and legal input before any pilot, plus one training centre to pilot with.",
 "Slide 17.",
 "A clear ask is the best close."),
("F5","What would you do with three more months?",
 "Month one: decide delivery for the first one or two sources and integrate them. Month two: move matching and mapping to a model with an approval screen, and build the evaluation set with educators. Month three: add consent and sources screens and the team view, then prepare a pilot at one training centre.",
 "Slide 16.",
 "Treat these as phases, not commitments, until data access is known."),
]),
("G","Curveballs and ethics","","Questions that test judgement. Short, honest answers work best.",[
("G1","What happens if the AI or the network fails right now?",
 "The toggle at the top of the chat switches to Offline rules, which answer from the same records with the same citation checks, so the demo and the answers continue. In Live AI mode, a model failure falls back to rules automatically and says so.",
 "Click ‘Offline rules’ and ask the same question again.",
 "This is the recovery play. Practise it once."),
("G2","Why not just build on Samaritan AI in SimCapture?",
 "We would reuse it where it fits. Samaritan works inside simulation and debriefing. CORA works across programmes and sources, and covers things outside SimCapture such as AHA cards, PEPP and local learning records. We would align with that team and with its principles on fairness and compliance.",
 "Slide 15.",
 "We have not evaluated its interfaces yet. Say that."),
("G3","Could the AI be biased?",
 "Not scoring people removes the largest risk. Remaining risks are that mapping or summaries behave differently across roles, units or languages. We would test across roles and units, keep humans approving mappings, and monitor results. We have not run bias testing yet.",
 "Guardrails slide.",
 "Do not claim fairness has been verified."),
("G4","Could an employer misuse this to penalise staff?",
 "It is a real risk, and we designed against the obvious paths: no scores or rankings, evidence rather than judgements, and visibility for the learner. We would also set an intended-use statement and contract terms, and take it through legal review. We cannot control every customer’s behaviour, which is why the design matters.",
 "Guardrails slide, ‘Built for regulation’.",
 "Acknowledge the risk before defending."),
("G5","Won’t people game the system?",
 "There is no score to game. The passport shows evidence that comes from the source systems, such as a manikin session or a provider card, not from self-reporting. Where self-reported evidence is allowed, it is marked unverified until an administrator approves it.",
 "Evidence drawer: source, assessor and validity basis.",
 "Self-report handling is part of the design, not the prototype."),
("G6","Does it work in other languages?",
 "The live model should handle questions in other languages, and the administrator search is written to accept them. The interface is English only, and we have not tested other languages. The offline rules are English only.",
 "Try one question in another language in Live AI mode if you want to show it.",
 "If you have not tried it first, do not demo it."),
("G7","What about data protection rules such as GDPR?",
 "This is staff training data, which is personal data, even though it is not patient data. We would carry out a privacy impact assessment, define the legal basis with Laerdal privacy, keep personal email under explicit consent, store emails hashed in records and in a separate vault so deletion requests can be honoured, and process data in the agreed region.",
 "Detailed design: PII vault.",
 "Do not claim compliance. Say what you would do and who you would involve."),
("G8","What is the biggest risk to this project?",
 "Access to data. Without sources, the layer has nothing to show. The second is trust: if clinicians or educators feel it judges them, it fails. We handle the first by keeping intake flexible and starting small, and the second by not scoring, citing everything and involving educators early.",
 "Slide 17.",
 "A candid answer to this question builds credibility."),
]),
]

CHEAT=[
("Where is the AI?","Live today: cited summaries, ask-this-passport, plain-English admin search. Rules stand in for matching and mapping. AI is for understanding messy data and explaining it.","A1"),
("Hallucination?","Only that person’s structured records, a citation on every claim, page checks, statuses computed by code. Honest limit: checks prove citations exist, not that they are right.","A3"),
("Do you score people?","Never. It refuses to rank. Scoring could be high-risk under the EU AI Act, so legal review comes first.","A4"),
("What goes to the model?","Role, unit, statuses and records. No names or emails. Admin search sends only the question.","A5"),
("Overlap with SimCapture and RQI Analytics?","They are strong inside their own product. CORA is the cross-programme layer and would consume their data.","C2"),
("What is the impact?","Hypotheses with pilot measures. We will not invent numbers.","B2"),
("Sources undecided, so is it feasible?","The value is in the layer. Any route becomes one common record. Pilot with one or two sources first.","D3"),
("What is real and what is mocked?","Real: interface, live AI chat, guardrail checks. Mocked: source feeds, sign-in, exposure logs, matching and mapping on rules.","E1"),
("What do you need from us?","Introductions to source owners, test data, AI services approval, educators, legal input, one pilot site.","F4"),
("The AI or Wi-Fi fails?","Click Offline rules. Same records, same checks. It also falls back automatically.","G1"),
]
